This internship introduced me to a wide range of tools and reinforced strong
software engineering and development practices. It gave me the opportunity to
contribute to a framework with real-world applications. I particularly valued
learning how to navigate and contribute to a large-scale software project,
discussing features, collaborating with the team, and seeing that my code was
not only useful but also reusable. I quickly grew comfortable in the
contribution environment, felt encouraged to ask questions, and was given the
freedom to take initiative.

Many tasks were completed, documenting resources for learning trame, doing trame
maintenance, developing a trame widget with examples, develop a trame-based DICOM
viewer for mobile.

Throughout the internship, I gained hands-on experience with a wide range of
tools and best practices. This included version control workflows using Git and
the Conventional Commits specification \cite{conventional_commits}, along with
Creational Design Patterns \cite{design_patterns} for software architecture. On
the frontend, I worked with \citev{VueJS}, HTML, CSS, and TypeScript, while also
utilizing \citev{PyPI} for package management, \citev{VitePress} for
documentation, and GitHub CLI \cite{gh-cli} for repository management.
Additionally, I interacted with \citev{GraphQL} APIs, automated project setup
with \citev{Cookiecutter}, implemented CI/CD pipelines via GitHub Actions
\cite{github-actions}, enforced code quality with \citev{Ruff}, and
containerized applications using \citev{Docker}. I also leveraged git
\citev{precommit} hooks, explored 3D Slicer \cite{Slicer}, and—of
course—deepened my expertise with \citev{trame}.